.TH PTOP 1 "2026-08-31" "ptop" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ptop \- guided performance measurement and benchmarking for Linux servers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ptop
.br
.B ptop run
.I test
[\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIquick\fR|\fInormal\fR|\fIdeep\fR]
[\fB\-\-tag\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR]
[\fB\-\-url\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR]
.br
.B ptop serve
[\fIADDR\fR]
.br
.B ptop info
.br
.B ptop history
[\fI#\fR | \fIdiff\fR \fI#1\fR \fI#2\fR | \fItag\fR \fI#\fR [\fItext\fR] | \fIarea\fR [\fImetric\fR] [\fIall\fR] | \fIrm\fR \fI#\fR|\fIsession-id\fR]
.br
.B ptop \-\-help
.br
.B ptop \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBptop\fR is a modern, \fBbtop\fR-style terminal benchmarking tool that walks users through server performance evaluations with sensible defaults, plain-language interpretation of every metric, and historical run-to-run tracking.
.PP
When invoked without arguments, \fBptop\fR starts an interactive terminal user interface (TUI) powered by Charm Bubble Tea and Lipgloss. For automated environments, scripting, or SSH sessions without a TTY, the non-interactive \fBrun\fR and \fBhistory\fR subcommands provide formatted CLI output.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (no arguments)
Launch the full-screen interactive TUI.
.TP
.B run \fItest\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
Execute one or all benchmark tests non-interactively, printing formatted metric values, verdicts, and historical deltas to standard output.
.TP
.B serve [\fIaddr\fR]
Start the lightweight built-in TCP throughput server on the specified address (default: \fI:5330\fR). Used as a peer for the network bandwidth test.
.TP
.B history [\fIargs\fR]
Browse, inspect, or manage historical benchmark runs saved in the JSONL history log.
.TP
.B info
Print a best-effort inventory of the current machine: OS and kernel, CPU model, core counts, scaling governor and notable CPU features, RAM and swap, transparent huge pages, NUMA nodes, whole-disk devices (model, size, HDD/SSD, I/O scheduler), network interfaces, and detected virtualization or cloud provider. All data is read from \fI/proc\fR, \fI/sys\fR, and \fI/etc\fR and requires no elevated privileges. It also prints the detected hardware \fBProfile\fR (storage class and compute class) and ends with a \fBRecommendations\fR section: plain-language tuning advice derived from the inventory (scaling governor, \fBvm.swappiness\fR, transparent huge pages, I/O scheduler, absent benchmark tools, and more), each with the exact command that applies it.
.IP
Disk metrics are graded against the expected range for the detected storage class (spinning HDD, SATA/cloud SSD, or NVMe SSD) rather than a single absolute scale, and the class range is shown alongside each value. The full inventory is stored with every history record and shown by \fBptop history\fR \fI#\fR.
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR, \fBhelp
Display help summary and usage instructions.
.TP
.B \-\-version\fR, \fBversion
Display version information.
.SH TEST SUITES
The \fItest\fR argument for \fBptop run\fR can be one of:
.TP
.B disk
Measures sequential read/write throughput, 4 KiB random IOPS, 99th percentile read latency, commit latency (fsync), small file creation/stat/deletion rate, and database transactions (SQLite ACID ops/s). At \fInormal\fR depth or deeper it also runs a queue-depth sweep (4 KiB random read at QD1 and QD32) and reports a storage-parallelism ratio - roughly 1x on a spinning disk or a throttled cloud volume, 10x or more on healthy NVMe. Prefers \fBfio\fR when installed, falling back to direct I/O with \fBdd\fR and built-in metadata and SQLite benchmarks.
.TP
.B cpu
Measures single-core and multi-core compute throughput, SMT core scaling efficiency, AES-256-GCM encryption throughput, compression rate, fork/exec process creation rate, hypervisor steal time, context switch latency, and thermal throttling (on deep runs). Prefers \fBsysbench\fR when installed, falling back to built-in prime computations.
.TP
.B mem
Measures memory write bandwidth, random-access latency (pointer chasing), available RAM, and NUMA node topology. Prefers \fBsysbench\fR, falling back to built-in memory copy routines.
.TP
.B net
Evaluates round-trip latency, jitter, packet loss, DNS resolution speed, TCP and TLS handshake durations, LAN gateway latency, network interface error counters, and network throughput. Bandwidth is measured against \fBptop serve\fR, \fBiperf3\fR, SSH, or a public HTTP CDN download. When executed as root, also estimates gateway flood-ping throughput without requiring an external peer.
.TP
.B gpu
Inspects installed GPU hardware, available VRAM, current utilization, and temperature (using \fBnvidia-smi\fR or DRM sysfs). Runs an OpenGL benchmark if \fBglmark2\fR is installed.
.TP
.B all
Runs all five benchmark suites (disk, cpu, mem, net, gpu) in sequence.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIquick\fR|\fInormal\fR|\fIdeep\fR
Adjusts test thoroughness and duration:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBquick\fR: ~10 seconds per test, ideal for rapid sanity checks.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnormal\fR: ~30 seconds per test (default), balancing speed and metric stability.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdeep\fR: ~60 seconds per test, providing maximum measurement stability and stress checks (e.g. CPU thermal throttling).
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-tag\fR \fITEXT\fR
Specifies a descriptive tag or label for the benchmark run (persisted in history).
.TP
\fB\-\-path\fR \fIDIR\fR
Specifies the target directory for disk benchmark read/write files (default: current working directory). Test files are automatically deleted upon completion.
.TP
\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR
Specifies a remote peer server for the network throughput test. The peer should be running \fBptop serve\fR, \fBiperf3 \-s\fR, or be reachable via SSH with public key authentication.
.TP
\fB\-\-url\fR \fIURL\fR
Specifies a custom HTTP/HTTPS URL for the network download throughput test.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR
Specifies the TCP port of \fBptop serve\fR on the remote host (default: \fI5330\fR).
.SH INTERACTIVE TUI
The interactive interface is a keyboard-driven workflow: \fBmenu\fR -> \fBsettings\fR -> \fBrunning\fR -> \fBresults\fR, with a separate \fBHistory\fR branch. The header shows a breadcrumb of where you are; the footer always lists the keys for the current screen, and \fB?\fR opens the full key reference.
.TP
.B Global keys
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUp\fR/\fBDown\fR or \fBk\fR/\fBj\fR: move. \fBEnter\fR: select / start / open. \fBEsc\fR: back one step. \fBq\fR or \fBCtrl-C\fR: quit.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBi\fR: open \fBAbout this machine\fR from anywhere - the hardware inventory, the detected performance profile, and the full tuning recommendations (the same content as \fBptop info\fR). The menu also carries the single most urgent tip.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB?\fR: toggle the key reference for the current screen.
.RE
.TP
.B Settings screen
\fBLeft\fR/\fBRight\fR or \fBh\fR/\fBl\fR change the highlighted setting (depth, target directory). \fBEnter\fR on the host field types a network peer address. The panel also shows what will run and any caveats; \fBEnter\fR starts the benchmark immediately.
.TP
.B Results screen
\fBLeft\fR/\fBRight\fR switch tabs between tests; \fBUp\fR/\fBDown\fR or \fBPgUp\fR/\fBPgDn\fR scroll. Each value is graded against, and annotated with, the expected range for the detected hardware class.
.TP
.B History
The runs list is the entry point (newest first). \fBEnter\fR opens a run, \fBt\fR edits its tag, \fBd\fR deletes it (with a \fBy\fR/\fBn\fR confirmation), and \fBc\fR marks a run then compares it with a second. \fBg\fR opens the trend charts: pick a test area, then a metric, for a Braille time-series chart and a run-by-run delta table (\fBh\fR toggles this-host / all-hosts).
.SH HISTORY MANAGEMENT
Every successful test run is persisted as a JSON record in the local history log file.
.PP
CLI history subcommands:
.TP
.B ptop history
List all recorded benchmark sessions with ID, timestamp, host, test depth, tag, and executed tests.
.TP
.B ptop history \fI#\fR
Display full results for run number \fI#\fR, including metric deltas compared to the run preceding it.
.TP
.B ptop history diff \fI#1\fR \fI#2\fR
Display a side-by-side metric comparison between run \fI#1\fR and run \fI#2\fR with percentage deltas.
.TP
.B ptop history tag \fI#\fR [\fItext\fR]
Set, update, or clear (if text is omitted) the tag/label for run number \fI#\fR or session ID.
.TP
.B ptop history \fIarea\fR [\fIall\fR]
Display all metrics for a test category (\fIdisk\fR, \fIcpu\fR, \fImem\fR, \fInet\fR, \fIgpu\fR) with sparkline trends. Append \fIall\fR to include results from all hosts.
.TP
.B ptop history \fIarea\fR "\fImetric\fR"
Display the complete run-by-run measurement history and sparkline for a specific metric.
.TP
.B ptop history rm \fI#\fR|\fIsession-id\fR
Delete a benchmark run from history by 1-based index number or session ID prefix.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PTOP_HISTORY
Override the filesystem path to the JSONL history file (default: \fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/ptop/history.jsonl\fR or \fI~/.local/share/ptop/history.jsonl\fR).
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directory for user data files.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/ptop/history.jsonl
Default history log file containing recorded benchmark runs in JSON Lines format.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Test execution failure, benchmark error, or history operational failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments, unknown flags, or non-existent benchmark runs.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start interactive benchmarking:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ptop
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run CPU and disk tests non-interactively with deep analysis:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ptop run cpu --depth deep
$ ptop run disk --path /mnt/fast-nvme
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run network test against a server running \fBptop serve\fR:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# On server (192.168.1.10):
$ ptop serve

# On client:
$ ptop run net --host 192.168.1.10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Browse and manage benchmark history:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ptop history
$ ptop history diff 1 2
$ ptop history tag 1 "baseline after upgrade"
$ ptop history cpu
$ ptop history disk "random read"
$ ptop history rm 3
.fi
.RE
